e>53</volume>:<fpage>40</fpage>-7.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15667374</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref17"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal">Rockwood K. Frailty and its definitions: a worthy challenge. <source>J Am Geriatr Soc</source><year>2005</year>;<volume>53</volume>:<fpage>1069</fpage>-70.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15935037</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref18"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal">Song X, Mitnitski A, MacKnight C, Rockwood K. Assessment of individual risk of death using self-report data: an artificial neural network compared with a frailty index. <source>J Am Geriatr Soc</source><year>2004</year>;<volume>52</volume>:<fpage>1180</fpage>-4.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15209659</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref19"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal">Janssen I, Shepard DS, Katzmarzyk PT, Roubenoff R. The healthcare costs of sarcopenia in the United State. <source>J Am Geriatr Soc</source><year>2004</year>;<volume>52</volume>:<fpage>80</fpage>-5.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14687319</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref20"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="other">Field MJ, Cassell CK, eds. <source>Approaching death: improving care at the end of life</source> Washington DC: National Academic Press, <year>1997</year></citation></ref><ref id="ref21"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal">Patrick DL, Curtis JR, Engelberg RA, Nielsen E, McCown E. Measuring and improving the quality of dying and death. <source>Ann Intern Med</source><year>2003</year>;<volume>139</volume>:<fpage>410</fpage>-5.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12965967</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref22"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="other">Wong PTP. Meaning in life and meaning in death in successful aging. In: Tomer A, ed. <source>Death attitudes and the older adult: theories concepts and applications</source> Philadelphia, PA: Brunner-Routledge, <year>2000</year> </citation></ref><ref id="ref23"><label>23<